; The oversight system's two properties, re-asked as derivations (extension §2)
; for `modality-cross-check.sh`. One `possible`, one `live` — and the two are
; encoded with OPPOSITE polarity, which is the thing to understand before
; editing either.
;
; `possible` is POSITIVE: `conviction-possible` holds the situations that
; satisfy F, so non-empty means the docket can conclude.
;
; `live` (AG EF F) has no positive encoding here — extension §3 rejects a
; nullary relation, so "for every reachable situation …" has nowhere to live as
; a fact. It is encoded as its COUNTEREXAMPLE SET: `accountability` holds the
; reachable situations from which a concluded docket is no longer reachable, so
; EMPTY means accountability holds. Rewriting it as "the situations that can
; still conclude" would invert the oracle's verdict on every `live` property in
; the repo while leaving the cross-check green.

(load "ct.rules")

; Both claims, from one guard — F, then EF F, then the reachable situations
; where EF F fails. Non-empty ⟺ `holds conviction-possible`; EMPTY ⟺ `holds
; accountability`. A capture with no restoration would show up as a witness
; situation in the last set; because restore-watchdog exists, it is empty.
;
; The guard was written twice here, once with an `(init S) (reach S T)` premise
; and once with `(situation T)`. They were always the same set — the engine
; enumerates reachable situations and nothing else (§12.3) — so the library's
; `satisfies` spells the one that says so.
(satisfies conviction-possible (is docket.stage concluded))
(can-reach can-still-conclude conviction-possible)
(trapped accountability can-still-conclude)
